{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "64",
    "case_summary": "A 21-year-old white female, G5P4004, at 22 weeks gestation was transferred to our hospital for evaluation of worsening sepsis. She had no significant obstetrical, medical, or drug history. She initially presented to a local health facility with a three-day history of headache, nonproductive cough, and fever. She was evaluated and discharged home twice within three days. Her symptoms persisted and worsened over the next week, leading to her admission to another medical facility in septic shock. She received intravenous fluids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and a dopamine drip due to persistent hypotension. Laboratory results showed pancytopenia (Hb, 7 g/dL; WBC, 1.7 × 10^9/L; Platelets 53 × 10^9/L), and she received 2 units of packed red blood cell transfusion before being transferred to our facility. Upon presentation, she was somnolent but arousable, pale but not jaundiced, and had mild expiratory wheezes. Her vital signs were: temperature 36.2°C, pulse 88 beats/min, blood pressure 96/54 mmHg, respiratory rate 30/min, and O2 saturation 100% on 40% oxygen. Her pupils were equal and reactive to light, and she had no lymphadenopathy. Heart sounds were normal with no murmurs. Her chest was symmetrical with good air entry, and bilateral scattered rhonchi were heard. Her abdomen was soft and nontender. Fundal height was consistent with her gestational age, and fetal heart tone was 155 beats/min. Cranial nerves II through XII were intact. The patient was admitted to the Medical Intensive Care Unit. Intravenous cefepime, azithromycin, and levofloxacin were started after cultures were sent. On the second hospital day, she developed respiratory distress requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation. During an attempt to place a left internal jugular intravenous access, copious amounts of purulent material were observed.\n\nCT revealed suppurative mastoiditis with erosion of the mastoid bone towards the left transverse sigmoid sinus; secondary septic thrombophlebitis involving the left transverse sinus, sigmoid sinus, internal jugular and left brachiocephalic veins. In addition, bilateral pleural effusions and multiple bilateral patchy areas of focal airspace disease were noted.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
